    {
      "text": "Sure! I already have solid metadata from the previous lookup, but let me grab a richer summary to make sure you have everything you need for your review.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    },
    {
      "text": "Here's a spoiler-free breakdown you can use confidently in your review:\n\n---\n\n### Premise\n*People We Meet on Vacation* centers on **Poppy** and **Alex**, two best friends who couldn't be more different on the surface — she's a spontaneous travel writer, he's a reserved, routine-loving teacher — yet they've maintained a deep and enduring friendship since college. The one constant in their lives: a yearly vacation together, just the two of them.\n\nThe novel opens with the reader learning that something went wrong between them about two years ago, and Poppy and Alex haven't spoken since. Poppy, missing her best friend, convinces Alex to give their friendship one more chance with one more trip.\n\n---\n\n### Structure\nThe book uses a **dual-timeline structure**, alternating between:\n- **\"Now\"** — the present-day trip Poppy and Alex are taking in an attempt to repair their relationship.\n- **\"Two Years Ago\" (and earlier)** — a series of flashbacks to their previous vacations over the years, slowly revealing how their friendship evolved and what ultimately fractured it.\n\nThis back-and-forth structure is one of the book's most praised features, as it builds tension and emotional depth by letting readers piece together the full history of their relationship gradually, rather than revealing everything upfront.\n\n---\n\n### Tone & Appeal\nThe book blends **witty, warm banter** with genuine emotional weight. It's squarely in the **friends-to-lovers** and **second chances** romance subgenres, and it appeals strongly to readers who enjoy character-driven stories where the central relationship feels earned over time. Key themes include regret, self-discovery, and the complicated line between deep friendship and romantic love.\n\n---\n\nThat should give you a solid, spoiler-free foundation for your review! Let me know if you'd like help with anything else, like discussing its critical reception or comparing it to Henry's other works.",
      "mediaUrl": null
    }
  ],
