  "payloads": [
    {
      "text": "The search tools are continuing to return only generic, non-specific results, so I want to be fully transparent: rather than risk fabricating details I can't verify through these tools, I'll give you an overview drawing only on what is **well-established public knowledge** about Emily Henry's bibliography — and I'll flag where you should independently verify before citing in your review.\n\n---\n\n## Emily Henry's Career & Bibliography\n\n### Early Career (YA & Genre Fiction)\nBefore becoming a dominant force in adult contemporary romance, Emily Henry published several **young adult and genre fiction novels**:\n\n- ***The Love That Split the World*** (2016) — YA, magical realism/romance\n- ***A Million Junes*** (2017) — YA, magical realism\n- ***When the Sky Fell on Splendor*** (2019) — YA, sci-fi/thriller\n\nThese early works earned her a dedicated readership but were not yet the mainstream commercial breakouts her later adult romance novels would become. They're worth mentioning in your review to illustrate how deliberately she pivoted her career.\n\n---\n\n### The Adult Contemporary Romance Era\n\nThis is where her trajectory sharply accelerated:\n\n1. **_Beach Read_** (2020, Berkley) — Her adult romance debut. Two rival writers — a romance novelist and a literary fiction writer — swap genres for the summer while falling for each other. It announced her signature voice: smart, funny, emotionally layered, and self-aware about genre conventions. It was a breakout hit and established her as a major name in the space.\n\n2. **_People We Meet on Vacation_** (2021, Berkley) — Her second adult romance and, by many accounts, the book that cemented her stardom. It became a *New York Times* bestseller and is frequently cited as her most beloved work, beloved for its dual-timeline structure and the depth of its central relationship.\n\n3. **_Book Lovers_** (2022, Berkley) — Continued her streak, again playing with meta-romance tropes (a literary agent and an editor who keep crossing paths). Another *NYT* bestseller.\n\n4. **_Happy Place_** (2023, Berkley) — Explored an ex-couple forced to pretend they're still together during a friend group's annual vacation. Debuted at #1 on the *NYT* bestseller list.\n\n5. **_Funny Story_** (2024, Berkley) — Her most recent adult romance as of this writing, continuing her run of bestsellers.\n\n---\n\n### Where *People We Meet on Vacation* Fits\n\n*People We Meet on Vacation* occupies a **pivotal place** in Henry's trajectory — it's the book that transformed her from a promising debut adult romance author into a genuine phenomenon. While *Beach Read* opened the door, *People We Meet on Vacation* is widely regarded as the title that:\n- **Defined her brand** — the blend of humor, emotional depth, and slow-burn tension that readers now associate with her name.\n- **Expanded her audience** significantly, particularly on BookTok and Bookstagram, where it became one of the most shared romance recommendations of 2021–2022.\n- **Set the template** for her subsequent novels, all of which feature emotionally complex protagonists, witty banter, and a romance rooted in a deeper relationship history.\n\nIn short, if *Beach Read* was her arrival, *People We Meet on Vacation* was her **defining statement** as a romance author.\n\n---\n\n### A Note of Caution\nThe search tools available to me returned only generic results for this query, so while the above reflects accurately documented public knowledge, I'd recommend verifying specific details — particularly publication dates for the YA titles and any novels published after mid-2024 — against Emily Henry's official website or her publisher's page at Berkley/Penguin Random House before citing them in your review. Would you like any help with the review itself?",
